    "q-37848": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37848",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Consider the following matrices:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & a & -2 \\\\ -1 & b & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ find the value of $(a+b)^{2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "From the diagonal matrix $D$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=6,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=-1.$\n\nSo, the second column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$\n\nNow, we need to find an eigenvector that corresponds to ${\\lambda }_{2}=6.$ Computing $A-6I,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{2}I & =[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -6\\end{aligned}]-6[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ that we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 12 & -10 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-6){R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ -3 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 14 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has two pivot columns (the $1$st  and the $2$nd ones). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation we obtain ${x}_{2}=0.$ Substituting this into the first equation, we get  ${x}_{1}={x}_{3}.$\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $(a+b)^{2}=(0+1)^{2}=1.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
