    "q-37853": {
      "dom_id": "step-q37853",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "$$\nB=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe matrix $B$ has eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$\nFind a diagonal matrix $D$ and an invertible matrix $P$ such that $D={P}^{-1}BP.$",
      "worked_solution": "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.**  Notice that we are already given the eigenvalues of the matrix: \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{3}=4.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$ Applying our usual method, we get the following.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-2I & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-2I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-2)I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{3}=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-4I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-4I)x=0$ gives us the eigenvector ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ in the usual way, and this gives us the following:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
